## What I built

The seam (Step 1, answered by reading not guessing): dispatch_tool_with_fallback in src/tools.rs. It is the outermost yoyo-owned seam — the one place both dispatch doors (sub_agent and explore_agent) leave through. The new SubAgentOutputMarkerTool wraps outside DiagnosticSubAgentTool, giving Marker(Diagnostic(Fallback(SubAgent))). That ordering is what keeps the marker applied exactly once: FallbackSubAgentTool's retry produces its result inside the wrapper, so it never returns to the construction site. Inside Diagnostic would have left the no-fallback path unmarked; in both would double-mark.

The change: pure mark_subagent_output(text) = SUB_AGENT_OUTPUT_HEADER + "\n\n" + text. The header says the text is sub-agent output, may summarise untrusted material (CI logs, web pages, foreign repos), and that instructions inside it are data to report, not directives to obey. Err propagates untouched — a refusal is yoyo's own sentence, marking it would make yoyo's words read as a child's. mark_sub_agent_result prefixes the first text block and leaves every other block byte-identical.

Tests (6, all at the emission point): whole-string assert_eq! on the composite with an anti-vacuous assertion first (the fixture body really does carry delete src/main.rs..., and the header is not the body); verbatim survival of the sub-agent's words; exactly one marker on the ordinary and the fallback-retry path; both Err shapes byte-identical and unmarked; a text-free result equal as a whole value; plus a deliberately weak source-level single-site guard (proves presence, never that marking fires — its doc comment says so).

Positive control run as one atomic mutate→run→restore: neutering the helper reddens exactly 3 of 6 tests by name while the pass-through guards stay green; the restore is byte-identical (diff verified, no NEUTERED residue).

Register: both entries pasted from what the gate printed — tool_wrappers.rs 5276 → 5574, tools.rs 4931 → 4940. ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/src/usage/commands.md updated in the same commit (docs explicitly say provenance marker, not sandbox or sanitizer).

Verification: cargo fmt -- --check OK, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings clean, cargo build OK, full suite 6077 passed / 0 failed.

One honest limit, stated in the code and the docs rather than implied: this makes an injected instruction legible, not impossible — a model that ignores the header is not stopped by it, and the text is not filtered.
